---
import NoteLayout from '../../layouts/NoteLayout.astro';
import Tex from '../../components/Tex.astro';
---
<NoteLayout slug="autodiff-removable-singularities"
  tldr="Autodiff differentiates the program you wrote, branch by branch, so a fix for a removable singularity must be right for the derivatives too: a constant branch has zero derivative, a series that is second-order accurate in value is only first-order in the gradient and wrong in the Hessian, and a masked branch still runs backward."
  source="The running example is the rod stiffness K₂ from my elastic-rod paper (SIGGRAPH 2025, Section 4 and Appendix G), whose energy Hessian needs second derivatives of K₁ and K₂. The measurements of what automatic differentiation makes of it are new for this note.">

  <h2 id="program">The program, not the function</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/notes/removable-singularities">previous note</a> fixed formulas with removable singularities so that
    they return accurate values near and at the bad point. Now hand the fixed code to an automatic differentiation tool.
    It does not know what function your code computes. It sees a sequence of operations, takes whichever branch the input
    selects, and applies the chain rule along that path. If the operations are right but their derivatives are not, you
    get a right value and a wrong gradient, with no warning. Symbolic differentiation of the code has the same blind spot:
    it differentiates the formula you give it, branch by branch.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rod">A real example: the twisting stiffness of a rod</h2>
  <p>
    In the elastic-rod paper (<em>ANIME-Rod</em>), the twisting energy of a rod made of any isotropic material contains
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`K_2=\frac{\rho\,\partial_1\psi-\lambda\,\partial_2\psi}{\lambda^2-\rho^2},`} />
  <p>
    where <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda`} /> is the stretch along the rod, <Tex tex={String.raw`\rho`} /> is the scaling of the
    cross-section, and <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi(\lambda,\rho,\rho)`} /> is the 3D material in principal stretches. It is
    <Tex tex={String.raw`0/0`} /> at <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda=\rho`} />, which includes the rest shape
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda=\rho=1`} />, where <Tex tex={String.raw`K_2`} /> equals the shear modulus. Appendix G
    switches to a second-order formula below <Tex tex={String.raw`|\lambda-\rho|=10^{-3}`} />:
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`K_2\approx\frac12\left(\frac{\partial_{11}\psi+\partial_{22}\psi}{2}-\partial_{12}\psi-\frac{\partial_1\psi+\partial_2\psi}{\lambda+\rho}\right)+O(|\lambda-\rho|^2).`} />
  <p>
    The rod's energy Hessian needs the first and second derivatives of <Tex tex={String.raw`K_2`} /> with respect to
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda`} /> and <Tex tex={String.raw`\rho`} />. To have exact answers to compare against, take a
    compressible Neo-Hookean material; then the singularity cancels by hand and
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`K_2=\frac{\mu-\Lambda\log J}{\lambda\rho},\qquad J=\lambda\rho^2,`} />
  <p>
    with Lamé parameters <Tex tex={String.raw`\mu=1,\ \Lambda=1.5`} />. At rest that gives
    <Tex tex={String.raw`K_2=1`} />, <Tex tex={String.raw`\partial K_2/\partial\lambda=-2.5`} /> and
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\partial^2K_2/\partial\lambda^2=6.5`} />. Here is what automatic differentiation reports for
    three programs that all compute <Tex tex={String.raw`K_2`} /> correctly:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The formula as written</strong> returns <code>NaN</code> at rest. Near rest it is worse than it looks: its
      value keeps about ten digits at <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda-\rho=10^{-6}`} />, but its second derivative there is
      off by a factor of about ten, because differentiating the quotient twice turns a rounding error of roughly
      <Tex tex={String.raw`\varepsilon/d`} /> into one of roughly <Tex tex={String.raw`\varepsilon/d^3`} />.</li>
    <li><strong>A guard that returns the rest value as a constant</strong> when <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda=\rho`} /> gives the
      right <Tex tex={String.raw`K_2`} /> and derivatives of exactly zero.</li>
    <li><strong>The paper's switch formula, differentiated by autodiff,</strong> is the interesting one. (The paper uses it
      to evaluate values; this is what happens if you differentiate it twice.) Its value is accurate to
      <Tex tex={String.raw`6\times10^{-7}`} /> at the threshold, and its first derivative to about
      <Tex tex={String.raw`5\times10^{-4}`} />. Its second derivative at rest is <Tex tex={String.raw`7.75`} /> instead of
      <Tex tex={String.raw`6.5`} />, about 20% off, everywhere inside the band, including exactly at rest.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The last one is a general rule, not a quirk. A formula that is off by <Tex tex={String.raw`c\,d^2`} /> is off by
    <Tex tex={String.raw`2c\,d`} /> in its first derivative and by <Tex tex={String.raw`2c`} />, a constant that does not
    shrink, in its second. <strong>Every derivative costs one order of accuracy</strong>, so a switch tuned for the value is
    not good enough for a Hessian. You need a formula one or two orders better than the value alone requires, like the
    fourth-order coefficients in the shell paper, or a derivative rule of your own (below).
  </p>

  <h2 id="demo">Try it: forward mode</h2>
  <p>
    A small forward-mode AD engine (truncated Taylor arithmetic carrying a value and its first two derivatives) runs each
    program. The plot shows the relative error of the derivative autodiff returns; the table shows what each program
    reports exactly at the singular point. The second function in the menu is the classic one-variable case
    <Tex tex={String.raw`(e^x-1)/x`} />, for comparison.
  </p>
  <figure class="note-fig ad-demo">
    <div class="ad-controls">
      <label>Function <select id="ad-fn"><option value="rod">K₂ of a Neo-Hookean rod (ANIME-Rod)</option><option value="expm1">(eˣ − 1)/x</option></select></label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="ad-k" value="1" checked /> first derivative</label>
      <label><input type="radio" name="ad-k" value="2" /> second derivative</label>
    </div>
    <canvas id="ad-canvas" width="880" height="460" aria-label="Log-log plot of the relative error of autodiff derivatives for several programs computing the same function."></canvas>
    <div class="ad-legend" id="ad-legend"></div>
    <div id="ad-table"></div>
    <figcaption>All programs compute the same function away from the singular point; the switch threshold is
      τ = 10⁻³. Errors at full double precision are drawn at the floor; missing or infinite values are drawn at the top.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Switch to the second derivative for the rod and look at the dashed switch curve: flat at about
    <Tex tex={String.raw`0.2`} /> all the way down to <Tex tex={String.raw`10^{-12}`} />. Then look at
    <Tex tex={String.raw`(e^x-1)/x`} />: there, even the "stable" program built on <code>expm1</code> loses its derivatives,
    because the quotient rule creates a brand-new cancellation, <Tex tex={String.raw`(x\,e^x-\mathrm{expm1}(x))/x^2`} />. A
    formula that is stable in value can be unstable in its derivative.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tape">Try it: where the NaN comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Vectorized code cannot branch per element, so guards are written as <code>where(cond, a, b)</code>, which computes
    both branches and picks one. In the backward pass the unpicked branch receives a zero cotangent, and that zero is then
    multiplied by the local derivatives of the masked division. If those are infinite, IEEE arithmetic makes
    <Tex tex={String.raw`0\times\infty=\text{NaN}`} />, and the whole gradient is <code>NaN</code> even though the forward
    value was perfect. This is a well-known pitfall with <code>jnp.where</code> in JAX, and the standard fix is the
    <strong>double where</strong>: also feed the masked branch a harmless input, so its derivatives stay finite. Below is a
    tiny reverse-mode engine with a tape, on the one-variable example. Read the backward pass from the bottom up.
  </p>
  <figure class="note-fig ad-demo">
    <div class="ad-controls">
      <label>Program <select id="tp-prog"><option value="single">one where</option><option value="double">double where</option><option value="taylor">double where + Taylor branch</option></select></label>
      <label>x <select id="tp-x"><option value="0">0</option><option value="1e-9">1e-9</option><option value="0.5">0.5</option></select></label>
    </div>
    <pre class="ad-code" id="tp-code"></pre>
    <div id="tp-table"></div>
    <div id="tp-read" class="ad-read"></div>
  </figure>
  <p>
    With one <code>where</code> at zero, the adjoint flowing into the masked division is a clean zero, and it becomes
    <code>NaN</code> the moment it meets <Tex tex={String.raw`1/x`} />. The double where removes the <code>NaN</code>, but the
    selected branch is still a constant, so the gradient at zero is <Tex tex={String.raw`0`} /> instead of
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\tfrac12`} />: the constant-guard problem again. Only a differentiable small-input branch is right.
  </p>

  <h2 id="custom">The clean fix: tell autodiff the derivative</h2>
  <p>
    Every AD system lets you replace a function's derivative with your own rule (in JAX, <code>custom_jvp</code> and
    <code>custom_vjp</code>; in PyTorch, a custom <code>autograd.Function</code>). For a function with a removable
    singularity this is usually the right design: evaluate the value with whatever switch you like, and give each
    derivative its own stable formula, typically its own short series near the singular point and the closed form away
    from it. The "custom rule" curves in the demo do exactly this and are accurate everywhere. The price is that you own
    those derivatives and must test them: against finite differences away from the singular point, and exactly on it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checklist">A short checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Test derivatives exactly at the singular point</strong>, which for materials is the rest shape: that is where
      constant guards and too-short series hide.</li>
    <li><strong>Count orders.</strong> A switch formula accurate to <Tex tex={String.raw`O(d^b)`} /> gives gradients to
      <Tex tex={String.raw`O(d^{b-1})`} /> and Hessians to <Tex tex={String.raw`O(d^{b-2})`} />; for a Hessian, <Tex tex={String.raw`b=2`} /> is not enough.</li>
    <li><strong>Never leave a division by zero inside a <code>where</code></strong>; feed the masked branch a safe input.</li>
    <li><strong>Check derivatives of "stable" formulas too</strong>; the quotient rule can bring back the cancellation.</li>
    <li><strong>For anything that feeds a Hessian, write the derivative rule yourself</strong> and test it over a log-spaced
      range of distances to the singular point.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="refs">References</h2>
  <ul class="ad-refs">
    <li>Huanyu Chen, Jiahao Wen, Jernej Barbič. ANIME-Rod: Adjustable Nonlinear Isotropic Materials for Elastic Rods.
      <em>ACM Transactions on Graphics</em> 44(4), SIGGRAPH 2025. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/3731208">doi:10.1145/3731208</a></li>
    <li>Huanyu Chen, Yumeng He, Jernej Barbič. Beyond Kirchhoff-Love: A Volumetric Approach to Thin-Shell Mechanics.
      <em>ACM Transactions on Graphics</em> 45(6), SIGGRAPH Asia 2026.</li>
  </ul>
</NoteLayout>

<style is:global>
  .ad-demo canvas { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 6px; margin-top: .5em; }
  .ad-controls { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .5em 1.4em; align-items: center; }
  .ad-controls select { font: inherit; padding: .2em .5em; background: var(--bg); color: var(--ink); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 4px; max-width: 100%; }
  .ad-legend { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .3em 1.2em; font-size: .85em; margin-top: .5em; }
  .ad-legend span::before { content: ''; display: inline-block; width: 1.6em; height: 0; border-top: 3px solid var(--c); vertical-align: middle; margin-right: .4em; }
  .ad-legend span.dash::before { border-top-style: dashed; }
  .ad-code { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 5%, transparent); padding: .6em .9em; border-radius: 4px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: .88em; }
  .ad-demo table { border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .85em; margin-top: .6em; width: 100%; }
  .ad-demo th, .ad-demo td { text-align: left; padding: .25em .6em .25em 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); vertical-align: top; }
  .ad-demo td.num { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; white-space: nowrap; }
  .ad-demo .bad { color: var(--link); font-weight: 600; }
  .ad-read { margin-top: .5em; font-size: .92em; }
  .ad-scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
  .ad-refs li { margin: .4em 0; }
</style>

<script>
  import * as A from '../../scripts/autodiff-removable-singularities.js';
  const cv = document.getElementById('ad-canvas') as HTMLCanvasElement;
  const ctx = cv.getContext('2d')!;
  const W = cv.width, H = cv.height, L = 70, R = 20, Tm = 16, B = 46, EMIN = -17, EMAX = 12;
  const css = (v: string) => getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(v).trim() || '#000';
  const STY: Record<string, [string, number, number[]]> = {
    naive: ['--muted', 2, []], rewrite: ['--ink', 1.5, [6, 4]], taylor1: ['--muted', 1.5, [2, 3]], taylor2: ['--link', 2, [6, 4]], custom: ['--link', 3, []],
  };
  const FAMILIES: Record<string, any> = {
    rod: { programs: A.ROD_PROGRAMS, exact: A.rodExact, xlabel: 'd = λ − ρ  (ρ = 1)' },
    expm1: { programs: A.PROGRAMS, exact: A.exact, xlabel: 'x' },
  };
  const fmt = (v: number) => (Number.isNaN(v) ? 'NaN' : !Number.isFinite(v) ? String(v) : Math.abs(v) < 1e-3 && v !== 0 ? v.toExponential(3) : String(+v.toPrecision(6)));

  function drawPlot() {
    const fam = FAMILIES[(document.getElementById('ad-fn') as HTMLSelectElement).value];
    const k = +(document.querySelector('input[name=ad-k]:checked') as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const muted = css('--muted'), line = css('--line'), bg = css('--bg');
    ctx.fillStyle = bg; ctx.fillRect(0, 0, W, H);
    const a = -12, b = 0;
    const px = (x: number) => L + ((Math.log10(x) - a) / (b - a)) * (W - L - R);
    const py = (e: number) => { const l = Math.log10(Math.max(e, 1e-17)); const c = Number.isFinite(l) ? Math.min(Math.max(l, EMIN), EMAX) : EMAX; return Tm + ((EMAX - c) / (EMAX - EMIN)) * (H - Tm - B); };
    ctx.strokeStyle = line; ctx.fillStyle = muted; ctx.lineWidth = 1; ctx.font = '13px system-ui, sans-serif';
    for (let e = -16; e <= EMAX; e += 4) { const y = py(Math.pow(10, e)); ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(L, y); ctx.lineTo(W - R, y); ctx.stroke(); ctx.textAlign = 'right'; ctx.fillText(`1e${e}`, L - 6, y + 4); }
    for (let e = a; e <= b; e += 2) { const x = px(Math.pow(10, e)); ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(x, Tm); ctx.lineTo(x, H - B); ctx.stroke(); ctx.textAlign = 'center'; ctx.fillText(`1e${e}`, x, H - B + 18); }
    ctx.textAlign = 'center'; ctx.fillText(fam.xlabel, (L + W - R) / 2, H - 8);
    ctx.save(); ctx.translate(16, (Tm + H - B) / 2); ctx.rotate(-Math.PI / 2); ctx.fillText(`relative error of ${k === 1 ? 'first' : 'second'} derivative`, 0, 0); ctx.restore();
    const xs: number[] = []; for (let i = 0; i < 360; i++) xs.push(Math.pow(10, a + (b - a) * i / 359));
    const shown = fam.programs.filter((p: any) => STY[p.id]);
    for (const p of shown) {
      const [col, w, dash] = STY[p.id]; ctx.strokeStyle = css(col); ctx.lineWidth = w; ctx.setLineDash(dash); ctx.beginPath();
      xs.forEach((x, i) => {
        const j = p.f(A.variable(x)), e = fam.exact(x), got = k === 1 ? A.d1(j) : A.d2(j), want = e[k];
        const err = Number.isFinite(got) ? Math.abs(got - want) / Math.abs(want) : Infinity;
        const X = px(x), Y = py(err); i ? ctx.lineTo(X, Y) : ctx.moveTo(X, Y);
      });
      ctx.stroke(); ctx.setLineDash([]);
    }
    (document.getElementById('ad-legend') as HTMLElement).innerHTML = shown.map((p: any) => {
      const [col, , dash] = STY[p.id]; return `<span class="${dash.length ? 'dash' : ''}" style="--c:${css(col)}">${p.label}</span>`;
    }).join('');
    const truth = fam.exact(0);
    (document.getElementById('ad-table') as HTMLElement).innerHTML = '<div class="ad-scroll"><table><tr><th>program</th><th>value</th><th>first derivative</th><th>second derivative</th></tr>' +
      fam.programs.map((p: any) => { const j = p.f(A.variable(0)); const v = [j[0], A.d1(j), A.d2(j)];
        return `<tr><td><code>${p.label}</code></td>${v.map((x, i) => `<td class="num ${Math.abs(x - truth[i]) <= 1e-12 * Math.max(1, Math.abs(truth[i])) ? '' : 'bad'}">${fmt(x)}</td>`).join('')}</tr>`; }).join('') +
      `<tr><td><em>true value at the singular point</em></td>${truth.map((t: number) => `<td class="num">${fmt(t)}</td>`).join('')}</tr></table></div>`;
  }

  function drawTape() {
    const key = (document.getElementById('tp-prog') as HTMLSelectElement).value, x = +(document.getElementById('tp-x') as HTMLSelectElement).value;
    const t = (A.TAPES as any)[key];
    (document.getElementById('tp-code') as HTMLElement).textContent = t.code;
    const r = A.runTape(t.build, x);
    const name = (i: number) => { const n = r.nodes[i]; return n.op === 'input' ? 'x' : n.op === 'const' ? n.label : `v${i}`; };
    const desc = (n: any) => {
      if (n.op === 'input') return 'x'; if (n.op === 'const') return 'constant';
      if (n.op === 'where') return `where(${n.cond}, ${name(n.args[0])}, ${name(n.args[1])})`;
      if (n.args.length === 1) return `${n.op}(${name(n.args[0])})`;
      return `${name(n.args[0])} ${({ add: '+', sub: '−', mul: '×', div: '/' } as any)[n.op]} ${name(n.args[1])}`;
    };
    const cls = (v: number) => (Number.isFinite(v) ? '' : 'bad');
    (document.getElementById('tp-table') as HTMLElement).innerHTML = '<div class="ad-scroll"><table><tr><th>node</th><th>operation</th><th>forward value</th><th>adjoint (backward)</th></tr>' +
      r.nodes.map((n: any, i: number) => n.op === 'const' ? '' : `<tr><td>${name(i)}</td><td><code>${desc(n)}</code></td><td class="num ${cls(n.val)}">${fmt(n.val)}</td><td class="num ${cls(n.adj)}">${fmt(n.adj)}</td></tr>`).join('') + '</table></div>';
    const truth = A.exact(x)[1], ok = Number.isFinite(r.grad) && Math.abs(r.grad - truth) / Math.abs(truth) < 1e-6;
    (document.getElementById('tp-read') as HTMLElement).innerHTML = `Output <strong>${fmt(r.nodes[r.out].val)}</strong>; gradient dy/dx = <strong class="${ok ? '' : 'bad'}">${fmt(r.grad)}</strong>; true f′(x) = ${fmt(truth)}.`;
  }

  document.getElementById('ad-fn')!.addEventListener('change', drawPlot);
  document.querySelectorAll('input[name=ad-k]').forEach((e) => e.addEventListener('change', drawPlot));
  ['tp-prog', 'tp-x'].forEach((id) => document.getElementById(id)!.addEventListener('change', drawTape));
  matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').addEventListener('change', () => { drawPlot(); drawTape(); });
  drawPlot(); drawTape();
</script>
